                            <a name="comment2850"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: The Whirlwind
</h4>

<p>
Putting aside the obvious, important question of whether or not David fudged on the facts, the issue he brings up is a really interesting one: Is there a place in the paper for honest, nonjudgmental looks at potentially suspect people or activities?<br />
<br />
Look through those comments. Check out some of the words from the posters.<br />
<br />
"Condoning."<br />
<br />
"Glorifying."<br />
<br />
"Approve."<br />
<br />
I've never understood this. Why do so many readers think someone's appearance in the newspaper indicates some sort of approval on the part of writers, editors, whatever?<br />
<br />
No one ever says we should stop writing about the dude who killed his neighbor or the guy who robbed the bank because those things they did are bad things and not good things.<br />
<br />
(This, of course, is to say nothing of the often breathtaking lack of grammar, spelling and logical thought from the people who make it a habit of posting on these kinds of "forums." Seriously. Should we be worried about THAT?)
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:mkruse@sptimes.com">Kruse</a>
	

 at November 14,2007 14:59
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment2851"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: The Whirlwind
</h4>

<p>
I think Robinson&#8217;s comments are interesting and illustrate this new dynamic in journalism, namely, how the subjects of stories have an unprecedented ability to talk back or respond to what was written about them.<br />
<br />
Today, for the most part, any subject of a story can comment on the piece that featured them, and refute anything in said story. Which begs the question, who do we believe? I have a feeling we as journalists will tend to believe our own, because we know what goes into putting a story together. And we don't want to think one of our own will make something up to serve their story. Unfortunately, I think the general public will be much more likely to believe the regular person, for much the same reason.<br />
<br />
I don't know how we do it, but we have to make the public realize that someone liek Filkins (or any other writer/reporter) has an editor hanging over his shoulder, hopefully making sure everything is accurate, while  the story subject can simply post a few comments saying the reporter made everything up. When that happens, our credibility can be damaged whether it's warranted or not.<br />
<br />
I tend to believe Filkins on this one. Looking at both parties, and thinking about which one had the most to gain by lying (or spinning, as it seems Robinson was doing), it seems to me that Robinson would more likely not be truthful. He opened up to the reporter about his speeding habits, and maybe drove a little faster than he intended to, and then bam, it&#8217;s on the front page, but what did he expect? Filkins was there to do a story on how the guy wants to set a record for driving fast.<br />
<br />
In the end, this simply means that we will have to be even more careful to make sure we get things right. But I even wonder if that will be enough. 
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:mtullis@dispatch.com">Matt Tullis</a>
	

