                            <a name="comment2852"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: The Whirlwind
</h4>

<p>
Thanks for the feedback.  <br />
<br />
As you may have seen in the post from my editor in our read &amp; react blog, Robinson sent me an e-mail thanking me for the story the day it was published.  A few days later, once the radio hosts and callers started slamming him, he changed his story and said I was lying.  <br />
<br />
Reporter who took notes at the time and risks getting fired if he lies vs. adult who lives in his mom's basement, has had his license suspended five times and has nothing to lose by attempting to deflect blame.<br />
<br />
Draw your own conclusions.<br />
<br />
Two things I probably should have done differently in retrospect.<br />
<br />
1.  The tone probably should have suggested more danger and less excitement - though I don't think this would have made the feedback any less hostile.<br />
<br />
2.  I never said how I knew he was going 160 since the speedometer stopped at 110. It's an important detail that would have cleared up a major point of the criticism.  It's a little late but for anyone who's wondering ... Robinson maxed his truck out during our ride and said he knew it topped out at 160 because a friend on a motorcyle clocked him the week before.<br />
<br />
I think most of the negetive feedback came from the fact that this sort of story isn't usually found in a newspaper.  I recently read a similar story in New York Magazine about a drug dealer and I'm sure they didn't catch loads of grief for it.  Like Kruse said, I'm not sure why as newspaper writers we're expected to be objective unless the subject is a bad person.  The next time I profile a clown who makes balloons for kids in the cancer ward should I find an anti-clown advocate to "balance" the story?<br />
<br />
<br />
<br />
<br />
<br />

</p>
